Binary Tree: A binary tree is a hierarchical data structure where each node can have at most two children

Root Node: The topmost node of the tree is called the root.
Children: Nodes directly connected below another node are called children.
Leaf Node: A node having no children is called a leaf node.

Consider this tree:

         A
       /   \
      B     C
     / \
    D   E

Subtree
A subtree is a smaller tree formed from any node and all of its children below it.

Example:
The subtree of B is:

      B
     / \
    D   E


Ancestor
An ancestor of a node is any node that comes above it in the path from root.

For node E:

B is an ancestor
A is also an ancestor

Because path is:

A → B → E

So all nodes before E in this path are ancestors.

Types of Binary tree
=======================

1) Full Binary Tree: Every node either has 0 or 2 children.

2) Complete Binary Tree (CBT)

A binary tree is called a Complete Binary Tree when:

All levels are completely filled
except possibly the last level.
In the last level, nodes are filled from left to right without gaps.

Easy intuition:
Nodes should not “skip places” on the left side.

Complete Binary Tree Example

        1
      /   \
     2     3
    / \   /
   4   5 6

Why is this complete?

All upper levels are fully filled.
Last level nodes (4,5,6) are filled left to right.
No empty gap exists before 6.

So this is a Complete Binary Tree.

Not Complete Binary Tree Example

        1
      /   \
     2     3
    /       \
   4         6

Why is this NOT complete?
Because:

Left side has a missing node.
2 does not have right child.
But later 3 has a right child (6).

There is a gap before 6.

In complete binary tree:
you cannot leave empty spaces in between.

3) Perfect BT: all leaf nodes are at same level

Example of Perfect Binary Tree:

          1
       /     \
      2       3
     / \     / \
    4   5   6   7


Not Perfect Binary Tree Example

          1
       /     \
      2       3
     / \     
    4   5

4) Balanced BT: height of tree at max log(N) where N is nodes

5) Degenerate Tree (Skewed Tree)

A Degenerate Binary Tree is a tree where every parent node has only one child.

It behaves almost like a linked list.

Example:

1
 \
  2
   \
    3
     \
      4

Here:

each node has only one child
tree becomes long and narrow

So this is a Degenerate Tree.

Another example (left skewed):

      4
     /
    3
   /
  2
 /
1

Types of Degenerate Trees

Left Skewed Tree
Nodes continue only toward left.
Right Skewed Tree
Nodes continue only toward right.


Questions
===========
1) Inorder Traversal : (left,root,right)

code: class Solution {
    void inorder(TreeNode root, ArrayList<Integer> ans)
    {
        if(root==null) return;
        inorder(root.left,ans);
        ans.add(root.val);
        inorder(root.right,ans);
    }
    public List<Integer> inorderTraversal(TreeNode root){
        ArrayList<Integer> ans = new ArrayList<>();
        inorder(root,ans);
        return ans;
    }
}

T.C. -> O(N)
S.C. -> O(N)

2) Preorder Traversal : (root,left,right)

code: class Solution {
    void preorder(TreeNode root , List<Integer> ans)
    {
        if(root==null) return;
        ans.add(root.val);
        preorder(root.left,ans);
        preorder(root.right,ans);
    }
    public List<Integer> preorderTraversal(TreeNode root) {
        List<Integer> ans = new ArrayList<>();
        preorder(root,ans);
        return ans;
    }
}

T.C. -> O(N)
S.C. -> O(N)